// R11 residual left by the 2026-09-15 block-scoped release, CLOSED the same
// evening: an `arc` local declared inside a VALUE-position block (a block
// used as an expression) was not released at that block's exit. `emitValueInto`
// owned those blocks and kept its own mark without emitting drops, because
// the block's value may be the very local. It now calls `emitValueBlockDrops`
// after the tail is lowered into the destination: `a` is boxed, `r` receives
// a CLONE of it (`_cell_t0 = cell_arc_clone(a)`), `a`'s own reference is
// dropped right after (1 -> 2 -> 1), and `r` is released at the end of the
// `while` body. Measured 0 on both witnesses; it read 3000 on both before,
// and the gate went red on the old pin before the constant moved.
//
// This program could not be written before 2026-09-15: the typed form was
// refused (the typechecker typed every block as unit) and the untyped form
// emitted `int64_t r` and did not compile. Typing a block by its tail
// expression made it expressible, and the leaks README's rule is one fixture
// per measurable gap, so this pinned it the day it became measurable, and
// stays pinned at 0 now that it is closed.
//
// The `owned` form of the same program, `let owned s = { let owned t =
// make() \n t }`, was refused until b608d59 the same day and is now a move
// (`t` needs no drop); `arc` is the shape where the release itself matters,
// which is why this fixture is `arc`.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (arc is
// scalar-first-refused there, same as examples/arc.cell). Lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus loops (see
// examples/leaks/README.md).

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        let arc r = {
            let arc a = "leaked-value-block"
            a
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
